WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.600
Welcome to Day 6: Business Formation, Compliance, Projects and SOPs.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.900
We'll start with business structure and formation,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.262
then compliance and good standing.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:17.112
After that come privilege, privacy and compliance, and project leadership.

00:00:17.112 --> 00:00:22.475
The biggest block today is process improvement, and we'll finish with SOPs.

00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:27.000
By the end of today, you'll be able to guide business formation and compliance

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.525
decisions, protect privilege and privacy, lead projects when timelines slip,

00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:38.500
improve processes with a real KPI dashboard, and write SOPs that hold up.

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.150
Section 1 of 6: Business Structure and Formation.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.350
We'll cover choosing a business structure, forming the entity step by step,

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.875
and registering in more than one state.

00:00:52.775 --> 00:00:57.000
The business entity someone picks decides how exposed they are personally,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.088
how they're taxed and how much paperwork follows.

00:01:00.088 --> 00:01:03.825
You won't be the one choosing it; the attorney and accountant will.

00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:06.325
But you'll gather what they need to choose well.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.588
A sole proprietor is simplest but personally liable for everything.

00:01:11.588 --> 00:01:14.788
An LLC separates the person from the business.

00:01:14.788 --> 00:01:18.700
A corporation is more formal and suits outside investors.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.388
First, ask what the business is trying to achieve:

00:01:22.388 --> 00:01:26.175
liability protection, a certain tax treatment, growth.

00:01:26.175 --> 00:01:29.700
Next, list every state it will actually operate in.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.925
Then, compare the options against those goals.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.262
After that, think about the next two years.

00:01:37.262 --> 00:01:40.975
A structure that fits today might not fit after expansion.

00:01:40.975 --> 00:01:45.625
Finally, write down why the choice was made, not just what was chosen.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.688
Here's a situation to work through.

00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:53.050
A client set up as a sole proprietor three years ago is now being sued over a

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.150
contract.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.725
His house and savings are exposed.

00:01:56.725 --> 00:02:01.050
What would an LLC have changed, and what would the attorney want from you now?

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.250
Forming a company is a sequence, not one filing:

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.575
choose the structure, check the name, file with the state,

00:02:09.575 --> 00:02:13.275
get a tax ID number and write the internal rules.

00:02:13.275 --> 00:02:16.962
Miss one early, and later something urgent, like opening a bank account,

00:02:16.962 --> 00:02:18.525
suddenly can't happen.

00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:25.075
First, check the name is available and not confusingly close to another company's.

00:02:25.075 --> 00:02:28.950
Next, file the formation documents, and confirm they were approved,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.400
not just received.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.262
Then, apply for the federal tax ID, the EIN.

00:02:34.262 --> 00:02:36.975
The bank needs it before opening an account.

00:02:36.975 --> 00:02:40.275
Finally, complete the operating agreement or bylaws,

00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:42.675
even though the state never sees them.

00:02:43.425 --> 00:02:44.725
Try this one.

00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:47.700
The state approved the new company two weeks ago.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.575
The bank can't open an account, the operating agreement is still a draft and nobody

00:02:52.575 --> 00:02:55.100
knows who the registered agent is.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.150
Put the missing steps in order.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.062
Forming the company makes it legal.

00:03:01.062 --> 00:03:03.975
Making it work needs three more layers: federal,

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:08.025
with the IRS; state, with tax and labor agencies;

00:03:08.025 --> 00:03:09.150
and banking.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.275
And keeping business and personal money completely separate from day one is what

00:03:13.275 --> 00:03:14.800
protects the owner.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.350
First, get the federal tax ID straight after formation.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:24.350
It's free from the IRS and needed for almost everything else.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:29.262
Next, register with the state tax agency, and the labor agency if there'll be

00:03:29.262 --> 00:03:30.500
employees.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.625
Then, open a separate business bank account.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.550
After that, set up simple bookkeeping before the first transaction.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:44.375
Finally, if there'll be employees, register for payroll taxes before the first

00:03:44.375 --> 00:03:45.450
paycheck.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.750
Think about this one.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:52.375
Elias paid the new company's first vendor invoice from his personal account,

00:03:52.375 --> 00:03:56.262
'just this once,' because the business account wasn't open yet.

00:03:56.262 --> 00:03:59.275
What's the risk, and how do you fix the record?

00:04:01.175 --> 00:04:06.000
Without an internal rulebook, a company falls back on the state's generic rules,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.475
which are rarely what the owners wanted.

00:04:09.275 --> 00:04:13.638
An operating agreement for an LLC, or bylaws for a corporation,

00:04:13.638 --> 00:04:18.375
sets out how decisions get made, what happens when an owner leaves and how disputes

00:04:18.375 --> 00:04:19.525
are settled.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:22.950
It isn't filed with the state, but it's the first thing a lender,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.600
investor or court asks to see.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.875
First, make sure it covers ownership shares and voting.

00:04:30.875 --> 00:04:35.175
Next, get it properly signed by every owner, not just drafted.

00:04:35.175 --> 00:04:39.800
Finally, keep the signed original safe, with the other formation records.

00:04:40.575 --> 00:04:42.112
Here's one to work through.

00:04:42.112 --> 00:04:46.138
A partner emails the other owners: 'I'm stepping back to 20 percent,

00:04:46.138 --> 00:04:51.800
OK?' Everyone replies 'OK.' The operating agreement still says 50-50.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.050
Is the change done?

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.500
What has to happen?

00:04:56.425 --> 00:05:01.575
Hiring just one employee in a new state can mean the company has to register there.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:06.325
A company is only automatically allowed to do business in the state where it was

00:05:06.325 --> 00:05:07.338
formed.

00:05:07.338 --> 00:05:12.375
Anywhere else, it needs a separate registration called foreign qualification.

00:05:12.375 --> 00:05:17.512
And an employee, a lease or a regular presence can all count as doing business.

00:05:17.512 --> 00:05:21.850
Without it, the company may not be able to enforce its contracts there.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.875
First, list every state where the company has a presence.

00:05:25.875 --> 00:05:29.162
Next, file a certificate of authority in each one.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:32.250
Then, appoint a registered agent in each.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.300
Finally, track each state's own annual reports and fees.

00:05:37.075 --> 00:05:38.475
Think about this one.

00:05:38.475 --> 00:05:43.162
The firm just hired a remote employee in a state where it has never operated.

00:05:43.162 --> 00:05:47.000
What needs to happen before their start date, and who do you involve?

00:05:48.925 --> 00:05:52.650
Section 2 of 6: Compliance and Good Standing.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.725
We'll look at staying in good standing, annual report and franchise tax deadlines,

00:05:57.725 --> 00:06:00.675
and keeping the company's records and minute books.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:06.175
Good standing means the state considers the company up to date,

00:06:06.175 --> 00:06:07.850
and it's never finished.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:11.525
Lose it, and the company can be blocked from signing contracts,

00:06:11.525 --> 00:06:15.275
from some bank actions and even from suing in that state.

00:06:15.275 --> 00:06:19.050
It slips through missed renewals, not dramatic events.

00:06:19.825 --> 00:06:23.775
First, put every filing and renewal date on a recurring calendar,

00:06:23.775 --> 00:06:25.775
with reminders well ahead.

00:06:25.775 --> 00:06:30.312
Then, if something has lapsed, tell the owner immediately and start fixing it the

00:06:30.312 --> 00:06:31.525
same day.

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:36.525
After that, check whether the requirements have changed.

00:06:36.525 --> 00:06:40.062
Finally, keep a simple status record for each company,

00:06:40.062 --> 00:06:42.925
so a lapse gets caught in routine review.

00:06:43.725 --> 00:06:46.037
Here's a situation to work through.

00:06:46.037 --> 00:06:50.575
One of Elias's companies lost good standing in Nevada eight months ago because an

00:06:50.575 --> 00:06:52.875
annual report wasn't filed.

00:06:52.875 --> 00:06:55.162
Now it wants to sue a supplier there.

00:06:55.162 --> 00:06:57.575
What's the problem, and what's the fix?

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:04.150
Every state sets its own deadline for annual reports and franchise taxes.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:06.400
There's no single national date.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.350
Some states have grace periods; many don't.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.825
So treat every deadline as firm.

00:07:12.625 --> 00:07:17.700
First, build one master calendar with every state, its deadline and how to file.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.275
Next, set reminders well ahead.

00:07:20.275 --> 00:07:23.925
Finally, check the filing was accepted, not just sent.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:25.862
Try this one.

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:30.325
You submitted the California annual report online on the due date and assumed it

00:07:30.325 --> 00:07:31.450
went through.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.712
Two months later, the state says it was rejected for a missing signature.

00:07:35.712 --> 00:07:37.950
What should you have done, and what now?

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.875
Being properly formed as a company doesn't mean being licensed to operate.

00:07:43.875 --> 00:07:49.588
And licenses stack up: federal, state, county and city can each have their own.

00:07:49.588 --> 00:07:54.725
Operating without one can bring fines, closure or even invalid contracts.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.737
First, find every license and permit this business and location need.

00:07:59.737 --> 00:08:03.100
A general business license is often only the start.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:06.325
Next, track each renewal on its own schedule.

00:08:06.325 --> 00:08:11.075
Finally, keep personal professional licenses, like a lawyer's bar license,

00:08:11.075 --> 00:08:12.650
current separately.

00:08:13.375 --> 00:08:15.075
Take a second with this one.

00:08:15.075 --> 00:08:18.487
The firm is opening a satellite office in a new city.

00:08:18.487 --> 00:08:23.150
What licensing questions need answering before it opens, and who do you ask?

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.050
'We never received it' is not a defense when the company gets sued.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:34.325
A registered agent is the official contact who receives legal papers,

00:08:34.325 --> 00:08:36.675
like lawsuits, for the company.

00:08:36.675 --> 00:08:40.300
If their details are out of date and a lawsuit goes unanswered,

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.625
the company can lose by default.

00:08:43.425 --> 00:08:48.025
First, make sure the agent's address is current and someone is actually watching it.

00:08:48.025 --> 00:08:52.462
Next, when papers arrive, send them to the right person immediately.

00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:54.500
They usually come with a deadline.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.100
Finally, update the state whenever the agent changes.

00:08:58.875 --> 00:09:00.250
Think about this one.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:05.725
The agent service emails a scanned lawsuit on a Friday at 5 p.m. The attorney who

00:09:05.725 --> 00:09:08.475
handles it is on holiday until Wednesday.

00:09:08.475 --> 00:09:10.775
What do you do before you leave the office?

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:17.362
A contract with the wrong signer or a missing notary stamp can be worth nothing,

00:09:17.362 --> 00:09:19.175
however good the terms are.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.537
'Executing' a document just means signing it so it takes effect.

00:09:23.537 --> 00:09:27.300
E-signatures, like DocuSign, are valid for most business documents,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.562
but some have special rules, so the attorney tells us which method to use.

00:09:31.562 --> 00:09:35.150
A notary checks the signer's identity and that they're signing willingly,

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.350
in person, or by video where the state allows it.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.325
And a company signs through a person with authority:

00:09:41.325 --> 00:09:45.975
'Thorne Advisory LLC, By: Elias Thorne, Managing Member'.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.575
First, before any signing, we confirm with the attorney who signs,

00:09:51.575 --> 00:09:56.475
in what role, by which method, and whether a notary or witnesses are needed.

00:09:56.475 --> 00:10:00.762
Next, we prepare the execution version: correct names and titles,

00:10:00.762 --> 00:10:03.750
date lines and a tab at every signature.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.600
Then, for a notary, we book it and remind the signer to bring photo ID and not to

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:09.900
sign early.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.637
After that, we check every page: signatures, initials,

00:10:15.637 --> 00:10:18.150
dates, stamps and attachments.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.338
Finally, we save the executed copy as read-only,

00:10:21.338 --> 00:10:24.700
send it to every party and calendar what it requires.

00:10:25.475 --> 00:10:27.212
Here's one to work through.

00:10:27.212 --> 00:10:31.400
Elias must sign a real estate document that needs notarization,

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.537
and he's traveling for three days.

00:10:33.537 --> 00:10:35.312
The deadline is Friday.

00:10:35.312 --> 00:10:39.075
What are your options, and what do you confirm with the attorney first?

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:45.800
Write down big company decisions when they happen, not when someone asks for proof.

00:10:46.575 --> 00:10:49.312
A minute book is the company's official history:

00:10:49.312 --> 00:10:54.050
formation papers, ownership records, meeting minutes and resolutions.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.988
Buyers, lenders and courts ask for it.

00:10:56.988 --> 00:11:01.750
In a dispute, gaps can make it hard to prove a decision was properly approved.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:07.375
First, record every major decision, like an ownership change or a new officer,

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:08.575
in writing.

00:11:08.575 --> 00:11:12.475
Then, store the book as carefully as the formation documents.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.488
Try this one.

00:11:14.488 --> 00:11:19.262
A buyer interested in one of Elias's companies asks for five years of minutes.

00:11:19.262 --> 00:11:21.525
You find notes for only two meetings.

00:11:21.525 --> 00:11:24.775
What do you tell Elias, and how do you start putting it right?

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:30.900
Section 3 of 6: Privilege, Privacy and Compliance.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.975
We'll cover attorney-client privilege, work-product confidentiality,

00:11:34.975 --> 00:11:38.050
and privacy rules like HIPAA and GDPR.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:44.450
Privilege protects private conversations between a lawyer and client about legal

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:45.475
advice.

00:11:45.475 --> 00:11:49.975
It's one of the oldest protections in law, and one wrong recipient on an email can

00:11:49.975 --> 00:11:51.137
destroy it.

00:11:51.137 --> 00:11:56.075
As an EA, you're inside that circle, so the duty applies to you too.

00:11:58.025 --> 00:12:03.200
First, treat any lawyer-client communication about legal advice as privileged.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:07.600
Next, check the recipient list before sending anything privileged.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.700
Then, don't discuss case details anywhere you could be overheard.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.137
Finally, if you're unsure, ask the attorney.

00:12:16.137 --> 00:12:18.325
A disclosure can't be undone.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:20.462
Think about this one.

00:12:20.462 --> 00:12:24.625
A client wants Elias's advice email forwarded to her accountant,

00:12:24.625 --> 00:12:28.250
'because he needs to know.' Forwarding it might waive privilege.

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:30.575
What do you do before sending anything?

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.725
Work product means the attorney's own preparation:

00:12:35.725 --> 00:12:39.675
strategy memos, draft arguments and case analysis.

00:12:39.675 --> 00:12:42.750
It's protected separately from client communications,

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.725
and careless sharing can lose that protection.

00:12:46.525 --> 00:12:50.025
First, handle it as carefully as privileged material.

00:12:50.025 --> 00:12:54.275
Next, never share it outside the matter team without clear permission.

00:12:54.275 --> 00:12:57.600
Finally, label and store it as work product.

00:12:58.375 --> 00:13:00.062
Here's one to work through.

00:13:00.062 --> 00:13:04.687
A friend at another firm asks you for 'a template' and suggests you send the Harlow

00:13:04.687 --> 00:13:07.250
strategy memo with the names removed.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.450
What do you say?

00:13:10.425 --> 00:13:13.375
Investors can be told some things but not others,

00:13:13.375 --> 00:13:17.025
and some information needs approval before it's shared at all.

00:13:17.025 --> 00:13:22.150
So an investor briefing and confidentiality are two parts of the same job.

00:13:22.975 --> 00:13:27.625
First, confirm what's been cleared before it goes into anything investor-facing.

00:13:27.625 --> 00:13:31.950
Next, keep investor materials in access-controlled storage.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:35.062
Finally, when an investor asks for something new,

00:13:35.062 --> 00:13:37.700
escalate rather than answering yourself.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.050
Take a second with this one.

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:43.725
An investor phones you directly after a board meeting and asks,

00:13:43.725 --> 00:13:46.475
'just between us,' how the lawsuit is going.

00:13:46.475 --> 00:13:47.675
What do you say?

00:13:49.675 --> 00:13:53.912
Medical records in a case file are always the most sensitive thing in it.

00:13:53.912 --> 00:13:58.037
HIPAA is the US law protecting personal health information,

00:13:58.037 --> 00:14:01.312
and it matters whenever a case touches medical records:

00:14:01.312 --> 00:14:05.987
personal injury, workers' compensation and disability claims.

00:14:05.987 --> 00:14:09.825
The firm doesn't have to be a healthcare provider for it to apply.

00:14:11.775 --> 00:14:15.350
First, recognize when a matter involves medical records.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:20.350
Next, apply that care even outside health-related practice areas.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:24.775
Then, share medical records only with people who genuinely need them.

00:14:26.725 --> 00:14:31.075
Finally, ask the attorney if you're unsure what extra handling is needed.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:33.112
Try this one.

00:14:33.112 --> 00:14:37.775
A personal-injury client emails you their full hospital records and asks you to

00:14:37.775 --> 00:14:41.050
forward them to their chiropractor and their employer.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:42.150
What do you do?

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:48.475
Europe's privacy law follows the data, not the firm's location.

00:14:48.475 --> 00:14:52.650
GDPR governs how the personal data of people in the EU is handled,

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.550
even by a US firm.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.775
It gives people real rights, like knowing what's held about them and sometimes

00:14:58.775 --> 00:15:00.200
having it deleted.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:04.925
And US state laws, like California's, work in a similar spirit.

00:15:05.725 --> 00:15:09.550
First, organize records with people's data rights in mind.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:15.025
Then, check at intake whether a matter involves personal data from outside the US.

00:15:16.975 --> 00:15:21.475
Finally, flag possible privacy obligations to the attorney early.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:23.650
Think about this one.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:28.800
At intake, a new client mentions its customer database includes people in Ireland

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:29.925
and Spain.

00:15:29.925 --> 00:15:33.900
What do you flag for the attorney right away, and why at intake?

00:15:35.825 --> 00:15:39.200
There are other rules too: for public companies' finances,

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:43.400
for financial privacy, for student records, and each state's professional rules for

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:44.475
lawyers.

00:15:44.475 --> 00:15:46.362
We don't need to memorize them.

00:15:46.362 --> 00:15:49.312
What we need is an instinct: health, financial,

00:15:49.312 --> 00:15:54.150
children's and foreign data are special, and deserve extra care before we even know

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:55.700
which law applies.

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:02.275
First, handle those sensitive categories with extra care by default.

00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:07.800
Then, ask the attorney whenever a matter touches an unfamiliar regulated area.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:15.200
Finally, remember that lawyers' ethics rules on confidentiality always apply.

00:16:15.975 --> 00:16:17.687
Here's one to work through.

00:16:17.687 --> 00:16:21.825
A client matter involves credit card numbers from its online store.

00:16:21.825 --> 00:16:23.700
You don't know which rule applies.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:27.225
What do you do with the files in the meantime, and who do you ask?

00:16:29.175 --> 00:16:32.300
Section 4 of 6: Project Leadership.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:34.512
We'll look at leading a project under pressure,

00:16:34.512 --> 00:16:39.000
handling scope creep, and keeping stakeholders informed during delays.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:45.062
When a project slips, look for what's broken, not who's to blame.

00:16:45.062 --> 00:16:49.250
Public blame doesn't fix delays; it hides the real problem.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:52.450
Leading a project as an EA means finding the cause,

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:57.200
keeping people informed on a schedule and guiding people you don't formally manage.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:02.775
First, find the actual cause before you reassign anything.

00:17:02.775 --> 00:17:06.762
Next, give scheduled updates, even when there's no good news.

00:17:06.762 --> 00:17:11.238
Then, turn the executive's big-picture goal into concrete next steps.

00:17:11.238 --> 00:17:15.275
After that, lead through clear asks and reliable follow-through.

00:17:15.275 --> 00:17:18.287
Finally, support decisions with short summaries,

00:17:18.287 --> 00:17:21.075
risks and options with a recommendation.

00:17:21.825 --> 00:17:24.162
Pause the video and try this one.

00:17:24.162 --> 00:17:28.950
A partner, the IT vendor and Elias are each asking different questions about the

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.050
delayed file migration.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.250
Write one short update that answers all three.

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:36.875
Quick check.

00:17:36.875 --> 00:17:41.275
A project is behind schedule due to missed deadlines from several team members.

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:43.175
What's the best first response?

00:17:43.175 --> 00:17:47.325
Is it A, take over the late tasks yourself so the schedule recovers?

00:17:47.325 --> 00:17:51.762
B, identify the root cause of the delays before reassigning anything?

00:17:51.762 --> 00:17:56.800
C, share a list of who missed which deadline, so the team sees who's responsible?

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:01.350
Or D, push the final deadline back to give everyone more breathing room?

00:18:02.175 --> 00:18:03.450
Take a second.

00:18:04.225 --> 00:18:09.038
The answer is B: identify the root cause before reassigning anything.

00:18:09.038 --> 00:18:12.850
You can't fix a delay effectively until you know why it happened.

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:17.275
Some big jobs come round every year.

00:18:17.275 --> 00:18:22.525
Year-end billing, trial season, annual renewals and conference season aren't one-off

00:18:22.525 --> 00:18:23.688
projects.

00:18:23.688 --> 00:18:26.738
So they need a system, not a fresh plan each year:

00:18:26.738 --> 00:18:30.000
build the playbook once and start earlier each time.

00:18:31.975 --> 00:18:34.375
First, write the playbook the first time through:

00:18:34.375 --> 00:18:37.625
the checklist, timeline and owner for each task.

00:18:37.625 --> 00:18:40.025
Then, start before it's urgent.

00:18:40.025 --> 00:18:43.562
The most common mistake is starting at the same moment every year,

00:18:43.562 --> 00:18:46.000
even after it ran late last time.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:50.175
Finally, after each cycle, update the playbook with what went wrong.

00:18:50.925 --> 00:18:53.312
Pause the video and sketch this one.

00:18:53.312 --> 00:18:57.500
Year-end billing at Thorne and Partners ran late last December because partner

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.163
approvals were slow.

00:18:59.163 --> 00:19:02.025
What starts when, and who owns each part?

00:19:03.925 --> 00:19:07.538
Projects rarely blow up in one go; they grow one small,

00:19:07.538 --> 00:19:09.688
reasonable request at a time.

00:19:09.688 --> 00:19:11.138
That's scope creep.

00:19:11.138 --> 00:19:14.050
A written scope isn't there to refuse everything.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:18.600
It's there so each addition becomes a conscious choice with a visible cost.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.175
First, write down the original scope clearly.

00:19:22.175 --> 00:19:26.375
Next, when a new request comes in, call it a scope change and say what it costs in

00:19:26.375 --> 00:19:28.500
time, budget or people.

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:31.850
Finally, log every approved change and why.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:33.762
Try this one.

00:19:33.762 --> 00:19:38.350
A partner asks you to 'just add' a small survey to the seminar invite.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:40.863
It's the fifth small addition this month.

00:19:40.863 --> 00:19:43.750
How do you say yes while making the cost visible?

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:49.950
People can handle bad news about a timeline; what they can't handle is being the

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:51.188
last to know.

00:19:51.188 --> 00:19:54.812
A delay you announce early is a manageable problem.

00:19:54.812 --> 00:19:58.775
A delay they discover on their own becomes a trust problem too.

00:19:59.575 --> 00:20:03.750
First, flag the delay as soon as it's likely, not only when it's certain.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:07.663
Next, lead with the bottom line: what's late and by how much.

00:20:07.663 --> 00:20:09.200
Then explain why.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:12.700
Finally, always include a new timeline or next step.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.788
Pause the video and try this one.

00:20:15.788 --> 00:20:18.900
A client-facing project is running three weeks late,

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.275
and the last update was a month ago.

00:20:21.275 --> 00:20:24.725
Write the update you send today, bottom line first.

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:29.775
Section 5 of 6: Process Improvement.

00:20:29.775 --> 00:20:33.750
We'll cover Lean Six Sigma and DMAIC, root cause analysis,

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.200
and the KPI dashboard template.

00:20:38.125 --> 00:20:42.825
You don't need a certificate; you just need to know which tool fixes which problem.

00:20:43.675 --> 00:20:48.312
Lean is about cutting waste and keeping only the steps that add value.

00:20:48.312 --> 00:20:53.075
Six Sigma is about reducing mistakes by measuring what's going wrong.

00:20:53.075 --> 00:20:57.325
And the standard project management approach gives big projects a clear structure

00:20:57.325 --> 00:20:59.000
from start to finish.

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:04.850
First, if a process has too many pointless steps, think Lean.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.487
Next, if it keeps producing errors, think Six Sigma.

00:21:08.487 --> 00:21:11.337
Then, if you're running a big project end to end,

00:21:11.337 --> 00:21:13.800
borrow the standard project structure.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:20.625
Finally, match the framework to what's actually broken, rather than using all three.

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:22.812
Here's a quick sort.

00:21:22.812 --> 00:21:26.475
A filing process with four unnecessary approval steps,

00:21:26.475 --> 00:21:30.862
invoices with repeated number errors, and a six-month office move.

00:21:30.862 --> 00:21:32.650
Which framework fits each?

00:21:34.625 --> 00:21:39.075
Five steps turn 'this keeps going wrong' into a fix that actually sticks.

00:21:39.825 --> 00:21:43.000
The method is called DMAIC, and it's a sequence.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:47.275
The example running through it is invoices going out late every month.

00:21:49.225 --> 00:21:52.850
First, define: name the problem in one sentence and who it affects,

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:56.925
like 'invoices go out four to six days late each month.' Next,

00:21:56.925 --> 00:22:00.075
measure: get real numbers, not impressions.

00:22:00.075 --> 00:22:02.925
Then, analyze: find the real cause.

00:22:02.925 --> 00:22:07.225
Is it a missing approval, one bottleneck person or a broken handoff?

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:12.300
After that, improve: change the actual process,

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:15.225
by removing a step or automating a check.

00:22:15.225 --> 00:22:19.375
Finally, control: build something, like a checklist or reminder,

00:22:19.375 --> 00:22:21.150
so the fix doesn't fade.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:23.662
Here's one to work through.

00:22:23.662 --> 00:22:27.925
A new client-intake form has 40 fields, half of them never used,

00:22:27.925 --> 00:22:30.787
and the team keeps making data-entry errors.

00:22:30.787 --> 00:22:34.800
Which part is a Lean problem, and which is a Six Sigma problem?

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:40.725
When a process keeps failing, fix the process, not the reminder.

00:22:41.475 --> 00:22:45.975
Take a contract that sits for three days because one partner has to sign it and

00:22:45.975 --> 00:22:47.387
they're often out.

00:22:47.387 --> 00:22:49.712
Reminding them harder doesn't fix it.

00:22:49.712 --> 00:22:52.575
Building a backup signer into the process does.

00:22:53.375 --> 00:22:57.325
First, for a searching problem, like a document spread across four folders,

00:22:57.325 --> 00:22:59.775
agree one filing place for good.

00:22:59.775 --> 00:23:04.112
Then, run a small DMAIC on a real frustration: define,

00:23:04.112 --> 00:23:09.075
measure for a couple of weeks, find the cause, change that step and check it holds.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.162
After that, for a form that keeps coming back incomplete,

00:23:14.162 --> 00:23:18.000
find the one confusing field instead of rewriting everything.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.750
Finally, once the fix works, make it the new standard.

00:23:22.525 --> 00:23:23.775
Think about this one.

00:23:23.775 --> 00:23:28.525
You fixed that field, and errors dropped from 30 percent to 5 percent.

00:23:28.525 --> 00:23:31.687
Three months later, someone brings back the old form.

00:23:31.687 --> 00:23:34.225
What control step would have prevented that?

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:39.662
Lean names eight kinds of waste, remembered as downtime:

00:23:39.662 --> 00:23:43.112
defects, overproduction, waiting, unused talent,

00:23:43.112 --> 00:23:46.750
transport, inventory, motion and extra processing.

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:48.412
You don't need the acronym.

00:23:48.412 --> 00:23:53.000
What matters is the instinct to say 'this step is pointless' when you see it.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:57.700
First, watch especially for waiting, like approvals stuck in an inbox,

00:23:57.700 --> 00:24:02.025
and extra processing, like typing the same data into three systems.

00:24:02.025 --> 00:24:06.888
Then, practice Kaizen: fix small annoyances as soon as you notice them.

00:24:06.888 --> 00:24:08.750
Small fixes add up.

00:24:10.675 --> 00:24:14.900
After that, don't skip the control step, or the old problem creeps back.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:19.000
Finally, treat this as a way of thinking, not a qualification.

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:21.512
Take a second with this one.

00:24:21.512 --> 00:24:25.925
Every week, you retype the same meeting details into Elias's calendar,

00:24:25.925 --> 00:24:29.238
the room booking system and a confirmation email.

00:24:29.238 --> 00:24:32.425
What's the smallest Kaizen fix you could make this week?

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:39.412
Root cause analysis means not stopping at the first, obvious explanation.

00:24:39.412 --> 00:24:44.462
The simplest version is the five whys: keep asking why until the answer stops being

00:24:44.462 --> 00:24:47.675
a symptom and becomes a cause you can fix.

00:24:47.675 --> 00:24:51.400
Fix a symptom, and the problem comes back in a new form.

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:55.225
First, state the problem precisely and factually.

00:24:55.225 --> 00:24:58.425
Next, ask why, then why again, and keep going.

00:24:58.425 --> 00:25:01.350
Finally, check: if we'd fixed this cause earlier,

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:03.300
would the problem have been prevented?

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:05.050
If not, keep digging.

00:25:05.825 --> 00:25:06.962
Try this one.

00:25:06.962 --> 00:25:10.425
A court filing was late, and the team concludes 'human error,

00:25:10.425 --> 00:25:14.925
the assistant forgot.' As the reviewer, what questions would you ask to find the

00:25:14.925 --> 00:25:16.175
real cause?

00:25:18.125 --> 00:25:22.525
Automate the task, give it an owner, then standardize it, in that order.

00:25:22.525 --> 00:25:26.000
Write it up before automating, and you'll just have to rewrite it.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:29.925
Leave it without an owner, and it quietly becomes nobody's job.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:34.325
First, find a repetitive task you still do fully by hand.

00:25:34.325 --> 00:25:35.988
Next, automate it.

00:25:35.988 --> 00:25:38.650
Then, decide who owns it from now on.

00:25:40.575 --> 00:25:43.000
After that, write it up as the standard.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:47.100
Finally, track just a few real measures on a regular schedule.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:49.462
Take a second with this one.

00:25:49.462 --> 00:25:54.238
Every Monday you build a matter status report by hand from five spreadsheets.

00:25:54.238 --> 00:25:59.125
Apply automate, own and standardize, and name the one measure you'd track.

00:26:01.075 --> 00:26:05.200
A good dashboard tracks a handful of numbers, each with a clear target,

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:09.512
across four areas: executive productivity, client service,

00:26:09.512 --> 00:26:11.962
operations and legal compliance.

00:26:11.962 --> 00:26:14.900
But the real value isn't the dashboard itself.

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:18.675
It's looking at it regularly and doing something about what it shows.

00:26:20.625 --> 00:26:25.650
First, give each measure a specific target, like calendar accuracy of at least 98

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:26.650
percent.

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:29.712
Next, review the dashboard on a fixed schedule.

00:26:29.712 --> 00:26:32.775
Then, treat procedures as living documents.

00:26:32.775 --> 00:26:37.575
Finally, when a measure keeps missing its target, update the procedure behind it.

00:26:38.325 --> 00:26:40.088
Here's one to work through.

00:26:40.088 --> 00:26:43.088
Invoices are going out eight days after month-end,

00:26:43.088 --> 00:26:46.038
against a three-day target, for four months running.

00:26:46.038 --> 00:26:48.775
Before blaming anyone, what do you look at?

00:26:50.675 --> 00:26:51.425
Quick check.

00:26:51.425 --> 00:26:55.600
On the KPI dashboard, which category does 'Filing Deadline Adherence,

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.512
100 percent' belong to?

00:26:57.512 --> 00:26:59.600
Is it A, Legal Compliance?

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.738
B, Executive Productivity?

00:27:01.738 --> 00:27:03.400
C, Client Service?

00:27:03.400 --> 00:27:05.875
Or D, Operational Efficiency?

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:07.975
Take a second.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.375
The answer is A, Legal Compliance.

00:27:11.375 --> 00:27:16.600
Filing deadlines are a compliance metric, critical for legal-adjacent EA and PA

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:17.450
work.

00:27:19.375 --> 00:27:22.500
Section 6 of 6: SOPs.

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:27.400
We'll cover how an SOP is built and triggered, pairing screen recordings with text,

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:29.450
and keeping procedures current.

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:34.850
A procedure nobody knows when to use won't get used.

00:27:35.625 --> 00:27:40.600
An SOP, a standard operating procedure, is a step-by-step guide that makes a task

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.288
consistent, safer and faster.

00:27:43.288 --> 00:27:47.187
Good SOPs share the same parts: a title and ID,

00:27:47.187 --> 00:27:50.362
purpose, scope, definitions, the steps, who does what,

00:27:50.362 --> 00:27:53.475
linked forms, risk notes and a revision history.

00:27:53.475 --> 00:27:55.350
And crucially, a trigger.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:28:00.000
First, identify the task that needs standardizing, and why.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.925
Next, define the scope: which people, departments and task types it covers.

00:28:04.925 --> 00:28:09.012
Then, write the steps as a numbered list, a flowchart or a checklist,

00:28:09.012 --> 00:28:10.738
never as a paragraph.

00:28:10.738 --> 00:28:14.400
Finally, name the trigger, the exact event that tells someone to use it,

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.625
like 'whenever a new filing deadline arrives.'

00:28:18.425 --> 00:28:19.687
Try this one.

00:28:19.687 --> 00:28:24.400
A courier-delivery procedure has no owner, no version number and no trigger,

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:26.825
and was last updated two years ago.

00:28:26.825 --> 00:28:28.975
What do you add, and who do you ask?

00:28:30.925 --> 00:28:35.375
A short video shows the task; a written summary makes it searchable.

00:28:35.375 --> 00:28:38.650
Some processes are much easier to show than describe,

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:40.900
especially clicking through software.

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:44.762
But a video alone is hard to search, skim or update.

00:28:44.762 --> 00:28:48.350
Pair it with a short written version, and you get the best of both.

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:54.050
First, record a short screen video, ideally under five minutes,

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:56.450
talking through what you're doing and why.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:29:00.175
Next, straight afterwards, write the steps as a numbered list.

00:29:00.175 --> 00:29:04.475
Then, store both together, video link at the top, steps below.

00:29:04.475 --> 00:29:07.550
Finally, keep each recording to one process.

00:29:08.325 --> 00:29:09.750
Think about this one.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:12.750
The firm updated its billing software last month,

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:17.887
and a temp followed an old screen recording and submitted invoices the wrong way.

00:29:17.887 --> 00:29:20.475
What should have happened when the software changed?

00:29:22.375 --> 00:29:24.750
A procedure is never really finished.

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:29.175
It gets written, reviewed, approved, monitored and revised.

00:29:29.175 --> 00:29:33.687
Version control means everyone can tell which version they have and what changed,

00:29:33.687 --> 00:29:36.950
so an old copy can't keep circulating unnoticed.

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:41.600
First, schedule regular reviews, quarterly or twice a year.

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:45.225
Next, every change gets a new version number and a log entry:

00:29:45.225 --> 00:29:47.962
the date, who changed it and what changed.

00:29:47.962 --> 00:29:51.212
Then, update outside the schedule when something triggers it:

00:29:51.212 --> 00:29:56.075
a new law, a better method, an error, new software or confused staff.

00:29:56.075 --> 00:30:00.375
Finally, measure whether it's followed, with real numbers like error rates.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:02.262
Try this one.

00:30:02.262 --> 00:30:06.187
Two assistants are following two different versions of the new-client procedure,

00:30:06.187 --> 00:30:10.875
both labelled 'v1.' How did that happen, and how do you fix it?

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:17.350
Writing a new procedure is only half the job; the other half is getting people to

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:18.237
use it.

00:30:18.237 --> 00:30:21.850
People resist change most when they don't understand why it's happening,

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:23.150
or weren't asked.

00:30:23.150 --> 00:30:25.625
So a rollout needs its own plan.

00:30:26.425 --> 00:30:29.325
First, explain the problem the change solves.

00:30:29.325 --> 00:30:34.075
Next, introduce it properly, with a walkthrough or a short training moment.

00:30:34.075 --> 00:30:37.725
Finally, check afterwards that it's actually being followed.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.137
Here's one to work through.

00:30:40.137 --> 00:30:44.987
You updated the shared travel-booking procedure on Monday without telling anyone.

00:30:44.987 --> 00:30:48.000
By Wednesday, three bookings were done the old way.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.675
What would you do differently?

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:53.962
Time to practice what you've just learned.

00:30:53.962 --> 00:30:57.000
In the Business Compliance and Project Recovery lab,

00:30:57.000 --> 00:31:01.800
you'll check the firm's multi-jurisdiction licenses and KPI dashboard for what's

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:07.150
off-target, then draft a recovery plan for a trial team that's fallen behind.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:08.725
It's your turn now.

